            Every astrophotographer knows the post-session dread: you come home with 400 sixty-second sub-exposures from your star tracker or equatorial mount. You know that somewhere in that four-hour sequence, a high-altitude cloud bank drifted through, a 15-mph wind gust shook the tripod, or a satellite train plowed across the center of your framing.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you stack those ruined frames, they poison your final integration. But finding them usually means opening your files in a dedicated viewer, waiting for 45-megapixel sensor files to de-mosaic, and pressing the right-arrow key four hundred times while squinting at tiny stars.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a faster, mathematically sound way to cull subs without manual blinking.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>The 3 Major Killers of Astrophotography Subs</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before looking at how to filter bad frames, it helps to understand what ruins a sub-exposure and whether stacking software can automatically save you:
          </p>

          <!-- Defect Table -->
