          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>Why Stacking Software Rejection Can't Fix Clouds</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many beginners assume that modern stacking software like Siril, DeepSkyStacker, or PixInsight handles everything with <strong class="text-white">Winsorized Sigma Clipping</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sigma clipping works by evaluating each pixel coordinate across all aligned frames. If 30 frames record a pixel value of 1,200 ADU, and one single frame records 45,000 ADU because a satellite flashed over that exact pixel, the outlier is rejected.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">However, clouds are not transient point-source outliers.</strong> When thin cirrus clouds pass through your target:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Target light from your nebula or galaxy is absorbed and scattered (extinction).</li>
            <li>Local light pollution bounces off the water crystals, lifting background sky brightness across the entire frame.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Because this change affects the entire frame rather than a handful of outlier pixels, sigma clipping incorporates that cloudy frame into the average. <strong class="text-white">Including just 10 cloudy subs in a 60-sub stack can erase the contrast of faint tidal tails and dust lanes you spent hours collecting.</strong>
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>Why Manual "Blinking" Fails at Scale</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            The standard advice in forums is to "blink" your data. You open Siril's sequence viewer, Blink in PixInsight, or Adobe Bridge, and tap through your subs one by one.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, this method suffers from three critical flaws:
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
            <li><strong class="text-white">De-Mosaicing Overhead:</strong> Modern mirrorless cameras (Canon EOS R5, Sony A7R IV, Nikon Z7) produce 45MB to 80MB uncompressed RAW files. De-mosaicing and debayering hundreds of files takes 10 to 15 minutes of disk thrashing before you can even begin reviewing.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Slow Gradients are Invisible to the Eye:</strong> High-altitude cirrus clouds don't arrive like a thunderhead. They creep in as a thin haze that subtly reduces star count and lifts background levels by 15%. When toggling through frames 2 seconds apart, your eyes adjust to the brightness change and fail to notice the gradual contrast decay.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Human Fatigue:</strong> After reviewing 150 consecutive dark frames of the Cygnus loop, your eyes glaze over. You miss trailing stars on frame 234 and cloud haze on frame 310.</li>
          </ol>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>The Modern Solution: 2D Scatter Plot Culling</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            Instead of looking at photos sequentially in image space, plot every file in <strong class="text-white">metadata parameter space</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong class="text-white">X-Axis:</strong> Chronological Capture Time across the night.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Y-Axis:</strong> Relative Mean Frame Luminance (%).</li>
          </ul>

          <!-- Graph Screenshot Card -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] overflow-hidden my-8 shadow-2xl">
            <img src="/software/astroplot/blog/how-to-cull-ruined-astrophotography-subs/graph-clusters-raw.png" alt="2D scatter plot showing cloud dips and outlier sub exposures" class="w-full h-auto block" loading="lazy" />
            <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 border-t border-white/10 bg-[#0c121e]">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-[#00FFFF] uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">Visual Scatter Plot</span>
              <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1">Spotting Cloud Incursions Chronologically</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed font-normal">
                In clear skies, target light frames form a smooth, consistent horizontal band across the session. When clouds roll in, the data points plunge visibly downward below the main cluster. You can spot an hour of bad weather at a single glance.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            When plotted this way, atmospheric physics does the work for you:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Clear Sky Sub-Exposures:</strong> Maintain steady luminance and tight clustering within a 2% to 4% band.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Passing Cloud Bands:</strong> Create an immediate, unmistakable downward "V-shaped" dip on the graph.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Sudden Dew Formation:</strong> Causes a continuous downward slope in transmission over time as your corrector plate or lens fog up.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>How to Cull Bad Subs with AstroPlot RAW in 30 Seconds</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            <a href="/software/astroplot" class="text-[#00FFFF] underline hover:text-white transition-colors">AstroPlot RAW</a> was engineered specifically to solve this bottleneck. Because it reads raw EXIF headers directly across multi-threaded CPU cores without full debayering, an entire night's shoot of 500 RAW files loads in 2 to 3 seconds.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here is the step-by-step culling workflow:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">1. Drop Your SD Card Folder</h3>
          <p>
            Drag your raw card directory into AstroPlot RAW. The app immediately parses all exposure parameters and displays your session on the 2D scatter plot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">2. Identify the Cloud Outliers</h3>
          <p>
            Look at your Light cluster across the middle. Any points dipping noticeably below the primary band represent frames affected by cloud scatter or obstruction.
          </p>

          <!-- GIF Demo Card -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] overflow-hidden my-8 shadow-2xl">
            <img src="/software/astroplot/blog/how-to-cull-ruined-astrophotography-subs/astroplotraw2.gif" alt="Marquee selecting and hiding cloud outliers with H hotkey in AstroPlot RAW" class="w-full h-auto block" loading="lazy" />
            <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 border-t border-white/10 bg-[#0c121e]">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-[#00FFFF] uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">1-Click Culling</span>
              <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1">Marquee-Select Outliers and Press H to Hide</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed font-normal">
                Simply click and drag a marquee selection box over the degraded outlier subs and press <strong class="text-white">H</strong> (Hide) or <strong class="text-white">E</strong> (Exclude). They vanish from the active export sequence immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">3. Export Only Clean Subs for Stacking</h3>
          <p>
            Once the bad frames are hidden, click <strong class="text-white">Organize</strong>. AstroPlot RAW copies or moves only the verified clean lights into your destination <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/lights</code> folder (and sorts your darks, flats, and biases at the same time).
          </p>
          <p>
            When you feed these clean subs into Siril or DeepSkyStacker, your integration SNR is protected, registration finishes faster, and no ruined frames contaminate your master stack.
          </p>
